WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.390
- As Michael Myers spies on
his sister and her boyfriend

00:00:02.390 --> 00:00:03.290
at the beginning of the movie,

00:00:03.290 --> 00:00:04.460
you'll notice that the clown mask

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:05.950
actually belongs to the boyfriend,

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:08.340
or at least he found it there
and decided to incorporate it.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:09.173
He brings it up with him

00:00:09.173 --> 00:00:10.870
when the two decide to go upstairs.

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:12.530
This small detail may be the key

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:14.350
to understanding the entire film.

00:00:14.350 --> 00:00:16.120
The opening scene is shown
through the perspective

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:18.040
of the six-year-old voyeur Michael Myers.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:20.280
He's at an age where kids
are very impressionable.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.230
What a child sees at this age
can shape who they become.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:25.350
Children his age, also,
normally aren't capable

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.480
of understanding topics
of a mature nature.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:29.050
He sees the boyfriend put on this mask

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:30.070
and kiss Judy with it,

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:32.440
then goes upstairs to do
something in the dark.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:33.870
It is perhaps not the first time

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.180
Michael has seen something like this

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:36.470
without their knowledge of it,

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:38.010
but he wasn't supposed to see this

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:40.150
and he doesn't know any
better than to imitate it.

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:42.360
He grabs a phallic object
and takes it upstairs.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:44.500
He comes across the mask
and puts it on himself,

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.270
just copying what he had
seen through the window.

00:00:46.270 --> 00:00:47.103
When he enters the room,

00:00:47.103 --> 00:00:49.600
he looks at Judy then looks
at the recently used bed,

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:50.670
then back at Judy.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.320
This little camera movement suggests

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.700
that he probably has seen
the deed done before.

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:56.090
He uses the object against Judy,

00:00:56.090 --> 00:00:58.550
then, as he had seen the
boyfriend do, immediately leaves,

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:00.370
goes down the stairs and goes outside.

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:02.540
For the first time we see
Michael and he's wearing

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:04.710
the red, yellow, green,
and blue clown costume,

00:01:04.710 --> 00:01:06.830
which matches the red,
yellow, green, and blue shirt

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:08.440
that Judy's boyfriend had been wearing.

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:09.910
The trauma of that night's events

00:01:09.910 --> 00:01:12.300
had all spawned from a
bad imitation of an act

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.240
that the child was never supposed to see,

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.880
and this experience would shape
everything that would happen

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.310
when Michael returned 15 years later.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:22.110
To learn the meaning behind of
this and other iconic scenes

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.583
stick around to the end of this video.

00:01:25.646 --> 00:01:28.396
(menacing music)

00:01:30.747 --> 00:01:33.530
"Black cats and goblins
and broomsticks and ghosts,

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:35.740
covens of witches with all of their hosts,

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:38.040
You may think they scare
me, you're probably right,

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.450
black cats and goblins
on Halloween night."

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:42.000
Welcome to Things You Missed.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:44.460
Yes, I'm finally doing Halloween.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:46.540
Now as a disclaimer, there
are certain things that happen

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:48.980
in the Halloween sequels
that kind of recontextualize

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:50.690
what happens in this original movie.

00:01:50.690 --> 00:01:52.890
But for this video, I'm just
gonna analyze the original

00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:53.870
as a standalone movie

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:56.280
and you'll see why as we
get further into this.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:01:58.060
For this series, I'll also be joined

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:01.433
by YouTube's resident's
Halloween expert, Jimmy Champane.

00:02:01.433 --> 00:02:04.010
- What we now know is one of
horror's biggest franchises

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:05.800
started as a small, independent film

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.400
directed by John Carpenter.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:08.830
There's not much I can say in this section

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:09.940
that hasn't already been covered

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:11.150
on plenty of other channels,

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:12.560
but I think you might be surprised

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:13.870
at some of the things you missed.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:15.820
So let's jump back into them now.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:20.820
(knives sharpening)
(ominous music)

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.760
- One of the best parts about Halloween

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:24.210
is that iconic soundtrack,

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.060
which kicks off at the
very beginning of the film,

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:28.370
where we see the opening titles
next to a Jack-o'-lantern.

00:02:28.370 --> 00:02:29.950
You'll notice the cutouts
of the nose and mouth

00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:31.630
are connected by this slice.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:32.510
I've seen it suggested

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:34.510
that this makes it look like
somebody holding a knife.

00:02:34.510 --> 00:02:35.580
I can kind of see that,

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:37.870
but I'm really more interested
in the camera movement

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:39.220
which pushes forward all the way

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:40.830
into the Jack-o'-lantern's left eye.

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:43.260
I see this as a foreshadowing
of the climax of the movie,

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:44.520
where Laurie used a coat hanger

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.190
to poke out Michael's left eye
so she can escape the closet.

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:48.360
I mentioned how Michael's youth

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:49.810
may have impacted his development

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:51.390
when it comes to mature topics.

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.287
As the admirable Spencer Charnas puts it

00:02:53.287 --> 00:02:54.750
"Stabbing in the Dark".

00:02:54.750 --> 00:02:55.583
Think about it.

00:02:55.583 --> 00:02:56.810
There's more than one meaning there,

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:59.070
but both meanings are
intertwined by the matchbox

00:02:59.070 --> 00:03:01.410
that Michael finds after
breaking out of the sanitarium

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:03.530
and into the station
wagon that he hijacks.

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.820
It says, "The Rabbit in Red Lounge".

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:07.870
Rabbits are often used as an adult symbol

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:10.730
while red, well, you've
seen a horror movie before,

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:12.560
you know what red stands for.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.290
- Blood. It stands for blood.

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:17.180
- Yes. Thank you for your
insight, Jimmy Champane.

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:19.680
The horror genre has no
shortage of fictional towns

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.540
that fans would probably love to visit.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:22.680
Maybe you'd like a creamy bowl

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.280
of clam chowder in Derry, Maine

00:03:24.280 --> 00:03:26.460
or soak up some sun in Amity Island.

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:27.890
One of the most famous examples

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:30.180
is Halloween's own Haddonfield, Illinois.

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:31.740
Having lived in Illinois as a kid,

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:32.573
go Cubs, go,

00:03:32.573 --> 00:03:35.040
I was curious about where
Haddonfield is located.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.460
- I always liked this location a lot

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:37.880
because it's pretty close to the place

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:39.670
where I grew up in Southeast, Michigan.

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:40.990
- When attempting to pick up Michael,

00:03:40.990 --> 00:03:43.370
the nurse asks why they're
taking him up to Harden County.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:44.980
Haddonfield, Maine may
not be a real place,

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:46.210
but Harden County is,

00:03:46.210 --> 00:03:47.680
which means the story must take place

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:49.950
way down at the bottom tip of the state.

00:03:49.950 --> 00:03:52.780
However, both direct sequel
show police paraphernalia

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:55.320
from Warren County, which
is all the way to the west.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:57.860
While "Halloween 4" places
the town in Livingston County,

00:03:57.860 --> 00:03:59.570
closer to the Northeast by Chicago.

00:03:59.570 --> 00:04:01.210
I think the idea is that Haddonfield

00:04:01.210 --> 00:04:02.490
is just supposed to be a stand in

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:04.130
for any small town in America.

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:06.020
The kind of every town USA ordeal.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:07.890
- Of course, I don't think
there's anywhere in Illinois

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:09.980
that has outdoor schools or palm trees

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:13.070
so it's probably best not to
focus on the location too much.

00:04:13.070 --> 00:04:14.220
In fact, the name Haddonfield

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.240
actually comes from the
writer-producer of the movie,

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:19.010
Deborah Hill's hometown, which
was Haddonfield, New Jersey.

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:20.610
And on top of that, the name Michael Myers

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:22.120
comes from the European distributor

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:23.757
who put out Carpenter's previous film

00:04:23.757 --> 00:04:25.220
"Assault on Precinct 13"

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.350
and made sure it was an
international success

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:29.150
because, you know, naming
an immortal serial killer

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:31.670
after someone is the most
sincere form of flattery,

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:33.440
and on a completely unrelated side note,

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:35.400
I fully expect your first slasher villain

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:36.470
to be named after me.

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:37.650
- I guess nobody told that

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:39.240
to the character of Michael Myers though,

00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:40.560
because as I mentioned earlier,

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:42.020
he still thinks that imitation

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:43.660
is the most sincere form of flattery.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:45.080
Just as he did 15 years ago,

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:48.090
he finds himself a costume
before going on his next rampage.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:50.770
Once again, he chooses a
one-piece jumpsuit and a mask,

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:52.810
but this time the jumpsuit
is a mechanics attire

00:04:52.810 --> 00:04:53.820
instead of the clown suit,

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:55.990
and the mask is a heavily
modified Shatner mask

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:57.340
stolen from the hardware store.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:59.210
- Although or maybe
because William Shatner

00:04:59.210 --> 00:05:02.010
would have been instantly
recognizable back in 1978,

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:04.160
they made a ton of
alterations to the mask,

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:05.900
like painting the skin stark white,

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:08.360
dying the hair brown and
widening the eye holes.

00:05:08.360 --> 00:05:10.550
It basically removed
every identifiable feature

00:05:10.550 --> 00:05:12.130
from the mask, which,
if you think about it,

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:14.060
is perfect for a character named The Shape

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:16.720
who's literally the
absence of all character.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:18.400
- Given the psychosexual subtext

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:19.710
that I've highlighted for this character,

00:05:19.710 --> 00:05:21.320
it's probably no coincidence

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.200
that he chooses to
imitate a male heartthrob

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:24.620
from a popular TV series.

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:25.890
One who happens to have given

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:27.770
one of the most noteworthy onscreen kisses

00:05:27.770 --> 00:05:29.100
in television history.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:31.430
The psychosexual metaphors
do not end there though.

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:33.470
This theme touches many
aspects of the film,

00:05:33.470 --> 00:05:35.930
like in this scene where Laurie
Strode is walking to school

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:38.073
while singing a little song to herself.

00:05:38.073 --> 00:05:42.806
♪ I wish I had you all alone, ♪

00:05:42.806 --> 00:05:45.510
♪ Just the two of us ♪

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:47.230
- Notice how when
Michael steps into frame,

00:05:47.230 --> 00:05:49.330
we can hear his heavy
breathing against the mask

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:51.685
as he stares her all the
way down the sidewalk.

00:05:51.685 --> 00:05:54.770
(Michael heavy breathing)
(birds chirping)

00:05:54.770 --> 00:05:56.070
Of course, at the end of the movie,

00:05:56.070 --> 00:05:57.550
we would later find that her idea

00:05:57.550 --> 00:05:58.880
of being all alone with a man

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:01.940
is far different than his idea
of being all alone with her

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:04.070
or any of the other
babysitters he would encounter

00:06:04.070 --> 00:06:04.903
in the neighborhood.

00:06:04.903 --> 00:06:05.990
But before we get there,

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:08.530
there is an interesting scene
in Laurie's English class

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:10.070
that we can analyze to learn more

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:12.273
about Michael Myers' true nature.

00:06:13.117 --> 00:06:16.034
(thunder rumbling)

00:06:17.573 --> 00:06:20.240
(ominous music)

00:06:21.610 --> 00:06:25.860
- [Teacher] No matter what
course of action Collins took,

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:28.623
he was destined to his own fate.

00:06:29.689 --> 00:06:31.205
His own day of reckoning.

00:06:31.205 --> 00:06:32.410
(ominous music)

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:34.360
- As Laurie sits in the
back of her English class,

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:36.100
her teacher gives a lecture about fate

00:06:36.100 --> 00:06:37.390
and she asked the class to compare

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:39.230
two authors' different portrayals of it.

00:06:39.230 --> 00:06:41.270
Laurie answers that one author, Costaine,

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:43.050
saw fate as being related to religion

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:45.630
while the other Samuels saw
it as a natural element.

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:48.017
The teacher then expands
upon Laurie's answer.

00:06:48.017 --> 00:06:48.850
- [Teacher] That's right.

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:51.810
Samuels definitely personified fate.

00:06:51.810 --> 00:06:55.840
In Samuels's writing, "Fate
is immovable like a mountain.

00:06:55.840 --> 00:06:59.380
It stands where man passes away.

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:01.200
Fate never changes."

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:02.900
- After watching the movie a few times,

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:05.180
this interpretation
starts to sound familiar.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:06.540
It's almost as if the English teacher

00:07:06.540 --> 00:07:09.170
is talking about Michael
Myers who Laurie briefly spots

00:07:09.170 --> 00:07:10.640
out the window during the scene.

00:07:10.640 --> 00:07:12.830
Michael is the personification of fate.

00:07:12.830 --> 00:07:14.750
Michael is literally this immovable force

00:07:14.750 --> 00:07:16.440
that will end the lives of nearly anyone

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:17.540
that he sets his sights on.

00:07:17.540 --> 00:07:19.900
Throughout the movie, Dr.
Loomis raves about how Michael

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:22.110
has nothing but evil
behind his empty eyes.

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:24.040
He is the only one who fully understands

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:25.550
the threat that Michael represents.

00:07:25.550 --> 00:07:27.460
If Michael was the
personification of fate,

00:07:27.460 --> 00:07:29.840
then Loomis is essentially
the author Samuels

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.150
warning everyone that
fate is coming for them.

00:07:32.150 --> 00:07:33.650
- [Jimmy] Perhaps another
clue to this metaphor

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:36.130
comes from the name of the
character, Dr. Samuel Loomis,

00:07:36.130 --> 00:07:37.830
who side note is my favorite character

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:39.090
in the Halloween franchise.

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:40.830
The names of these two
authors seem fictional

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:41.663
or at the very least,

00:07:41.663 --> 00:07:43.360
they're not obvious to us as the audience

00:07:43.360 --> 00:07:45.860
when the teacher mentions the
name Samuels and Costaine.

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:48.090
Because of that, it's
extremely likely that Samuels

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:50.030
and Sam Loomis having very similar names

00:07:50.030 --> 00:07:51.440
was an intentional clue.

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:53.050
There's another potentially
noteworthy point

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:55.170
about Sam Loomis that I
think is worth mentioning.

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:58.250
If you look at Alfred
Hitchcock's 1960 classic Psycho,

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:00.140
there's a character named Sam Loomis.

00:08:00.140 --> 00:08:01.950
Normally, I don't think this
will be worth bringing up

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:04.040
in a video, but there are
a few other connections

00:08:04.040 --> 00:08:05.500
to Psycho in Halloween.

00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:07.210
So in Psycho, the character Sam Loomis

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:08.930
sleeps with the character Marion Crane

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:11.370
and in Halloween, there's a
character named Sam Loomis

00:08:11.370 --> 00:08:13.270
who hangs out with a
character named Marion.

00:08:13.270 --> 00:08:15.430
Her name is Marion Chambers,
but it's still Marion.

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:16.850
And then you got to
think about the fact that

00:08:16.850 --> 00:08:19.400
Marion Crane is played
by Janet Leigh in Psycho

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:21.540
and Janet Leigh is Jamie Lee Curtis's mom.

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:23.370
Jamie Lee Curtis plays the main character

00:08:23.370 --> 00:08:24.850
in Halloween, Laurie Strode.

00:08:24.850 --> 00:08:26.340
And finally the lighting in Halloween

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:28.320
is clearly inspired by
the lighting in Psycho.

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:29.630
There are many scenes in Halloween

00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:31.420
where you see characters striped in shadow

00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:32.920
like the light is coming through blinds

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:34.650
or other horizontal objects.

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:36.050
- [ZAC] Then there's a broader connection

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:37.450
with the film's antagonist.

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:39.830
Spoiler alert for Psycho
until the end of this chapter.

00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:42.020
Michael Myers and Norman
Bates are both disturbed

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:44.150
by mental conditions
acquired in their youth.

00:08:44.150 --> 00:08:46.540
They both seem to be affected
by an Oedipus complex

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:48.250
or at least some variation of it.

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:50.080
Norman develops this
complex for his mother

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:51.990
while Michael vents his
developmental issues

00:08:51.990 --> 00:08:53.350
into his sister, Judith.

00:08:53.350 --> 00:08:55.100
Although Judith was his first target,

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:57.010
all we really know is
that she's a teenager

00:08:57.010 --> 00:08:59.050
and it seems to be her
activity with her boyfriend

00:08:59.050 --> 00:09:00.170
that sets Michael off.

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:03.130
When he returns to Haddonfield
on Halloween in 1978,

00:09:03.130 --> 00:09:04.660
his attack pattern continues.

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:06.600
He claims for human victims in this movie

00:09:06.600 --> 00:09:08.350
and comes after a fifth, which is Laurie,

00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:09.680
but she's able to evade him.

00:09:09.680 --> 00:09:11.890
The first was the mechanic,
which was off screen.

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:13.360
We can't really analyze that one.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:15.650
The other three are
sexually active teenagers,

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:17.140
just like Judith was.

00:09:17.140 --> 00:09:19.610
In fact, these girls hardly
seem to think about anything

00:09:19.610 --> 00:09:20.700
besides hooking up.

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:22.100
At one point when Laurie mentioned

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:23.380
that she forgot her Chemistry book,

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:25.550
Lynda raves about how she
never brings her books home

00:09:25.550 --> 00:09:26.800
because she doesn't need them.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:27.633
She's a liar though.

00:09:27.633 --> 00:09:29.087
She does have two books with her;

00:09:29.087 --> 00:09:32.180
"One Love Forever" and
"Tender Love..." something.

00:09:32.180 --> 00:09:33.870
We don't really get a
good look at this one.

00:09:33.870 --> 00:09:35.570
The girl's priorities are clear.

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:37.130
Laurie prioritizes her future

00:09:37.130 --> 00:09:38.950
by wanting to study and get good grades.

00:09:38.950 --> 00:09:41.620
Lynda is more interested
in love and getting busy.

00:09:41.620 --> 00:09:44.040
You could say that they
represents the Liberation Movement

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:46.690
that was going on from
the 1960s to the 1980s,

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:49.030
where traditional codes of
conduct were being challenged.

00:09:49.030 --> 00:09:50.860
Laurie represents the
traditional point of view.

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:53.660
She's always covered up and
very modest about herself.

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:56.640
Her friends are, well,
the opposite of that.

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:57.880
They look for any excuse

00:09:57.880 --> 00:09:59.670
to be a little bit more promiscuous.

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:01.630
Have you ever seen someone
completely strip down

00:10:01.630 --> 00:10:03.350
and throw their entire outfit in the wash

00:10:03.350 --> 00:10:04.420
after a small spill?

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:05.690
When Annie is in her underwear,

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:07.370
Laurie is actually adding on layers.

00:10:07.370 --> 00:10:09.890
She puts on an apron while
getting a pumpkin with the kids.

00:10:09.890 --> 00:10:11.450
Annie has a cigarette in the car.

00:10:11.450 --> 00:10:13.890
She easily inhales it but
Lori chokes on the smoke

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:16.060
showing us that she's not
used to breathing in poison.

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:17.540
She is innocent and pure.

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:19.870
These girls are wired
with teenage hormones.

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:21.420
The difference is that Lynda and Annie

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:22.660
are acting on their urges

00:10:22.660 --> 00:10:25.130
while Laurie keeps them internalized.

00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:28.040
- God, I've got a shirt on.
That's all you ever think about.

00:10:28.040 --> 00:10:29.420
- [Paul] I think it's
all you ever think about.

00:10:29.420 --> 00:10:31.663
- That's not true. I think
about lots of things.

00:10:31.663 --> 00:10:34.130
Now, why don't we not stand
here talking about them

00:10:34.130 --> 00:10:35.700
and get down to doing them?

00:10:35.700 --> 00:10:36.880
- [Zac] Annie even teases Laurie

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:39.650
for not being totally consumed
with dating like she is.

00:10:39.650 --> 00:10:42.400
- Laurie dear, he wants to talk to you.

00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:44.730
He wants to take you out tonight.

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:45.610
- We come to find out

00:10:45.610 --> 00:10:47.330
that Laurie actually does have a crush.

00:10:47.330 --> 00:10:48.540
She just doesn't see herself

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:50.300
as part of this whole liberation movement.

00:10:50.300 --> 00:10:52.210
When Annie tells her she could
ask someone to the dance,

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:53.542
she literally says...

00:10:53.542 --> 00:10:54.950
- You could do that. I couldn't.

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:56.460
I even found myself wondering

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:58.900
if the book that Laurie
forgets, her Chemistry book,

00:10:58.900 --> 00:11:00.910
is an intentional comment
about her character.

00:11:00.910 --> 00:11:03.200
Her two friends have boyfriends
but Laurie is single.

00:11:03.200 --> 00:11:05.980
She hasn't developed that
chemistry with a man yet.

00:11:05.980 --> 00:11:07.140
- [JIMMY] Writer-director John Carpenter

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:08.520
and writer-producer Debra Hill

00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:10.120
have given many interviews over the years

00:11:10.120 --> 00:11:12.050
where they clarified that
it was never their intent

00:11:12.050 --> 00:11:13.670
to have the main character's survival

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:15.450
be mainly because she stays a virgin

00:11:15.450 --> 00:11:16.680
throughout the entire film.

00:11:16.680 --> 00:11:17.513
With that being said, though,

00:11:17.513 --> 00:11:20.130
they've also attributed Laurie's
survival to her alertness,

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:22.830
whereas her friends are
distracted with horny endeavors.

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:23.663
I guess they're saying that

00:11:23.663 --> 00:11:25.640
because it adds a little bit
of nuance to their writing

00:11:25.640 --> 00:11:27.510
and they are totally within
their right to do so.

00:11:27.510 --> 00:11:28.880
But to me, it's all the same.

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:30.980
It's kind of obvious that
Michael is trying to recreate

00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:33.510
his sister's final night
with other lust-driven teens,

00:11:33.510 --> 00:11:35.110
and it's Laurie's lack of beguilement

00:11:35.110 --> 00:11:36.730
that helps her survive till the end.

00:11:36.730 --> 00:11:37.860
And just for some quick trivia,

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:39.740
Jamie Lee Curtis has
talked about in interviews

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:42.070
that she was actually kind of
surprised that she was cast

00:11:42.070 --> 00:11:44.260
as the goody two-shoes
character of Laurie Strode

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:45.093
because in real life,

00:11:45.093 --> 00:11:47.160
she was actually more
like Annie and Lynda.

00:11:47.160 --> 00:11:49.490
- This makes sense if we
analyze Michael's behavior.

00:11:49.490 --> 00:11:50.323
When school first lets out,

00:11:50.323 --> 00:11:53.030
one of the elementary kids,
Richie, runs into Michael.

00:11:53.030 --> 00:11:54.930
There's an ample
opportunity to take him out

00:11:54.930 --> 00:11:56.400
but he just lets him run along.

00:11:56.400 --> 00:11:57.233
There were two moments

00:11:57.233 --> 00:11:58.840
when Michael seems to eye Tommy Doyle

00:11:58.840 --> 00:12:01.610
as he walks home from school,
but ultimately just moves on.

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:02.760
He's not James Charles.

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:04.800
He doesn't seem interested in little boys.

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:07.370
Although Tommy and Lindsey
are afraid of the boogeyman,

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:09.890
there's never a moment where
he actually pursues them.

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:11.460
It was always about sticking his knife

00:12:11.460 --> 00:12:14.120
into Haddonfield High School's
most promiscuous teens.

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:15.630
I mean, look how he's holding
the knife right there.

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:16.660
I mean, come on.

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:18.630
- It's never really been
confirmed one way or the other,

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:19.470
which I kind of like.

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:21.200
But the leading theory as to why Michael

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:23.460
is trying to go after
teenagers to be his victims

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:26.160
is because they remind him of
his sister and her boyfriend.

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:27.810
Oh, and he also thinks they're all unpure,

00:12:27.810 --> 00:12:30.190
which is why he can go after
Laurie Strode who's a virgin.

00:12:30.190 --> 00:12:31.690
- Loomis and Sheriff Brackett are worried

00:12:31.690 --> 00:12:33.420
about the safety of the
town's trick-or-treaters,

00:12:33.420 --> 00:12:35.180
but this never turns out to be a problem.

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:36.610
Even though this kid
doesn't have a costume.

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:38.010
I mean, what the hell kid? It's Halloween.

00:12:38.010 --> 00:12:39.330
You got to get yourself a costume.

00:12:39.330 --> 00:12:40.600
Michael's Freudian issues

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:42.310
aren't just about who he's going after,

00:12:42.310 --> 00:12:44.150
it's also how he's going about it.

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:46.440
But before we get there, there
are some important secrets

00:12:46.440 --> 00:12:48.504
to decipher in Laurie's room.

00:12:48.504 --> 00:12:51.421
(thunder rumbling)

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:56.177
(ominous music)

00:12:57.330 --> 00:12:58.730
When the girls get out of school,

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:01.127
you can hear the cheerleaders
practicing in the background.

00:13:01.127 --> 00:13:03.150
"We're from Haddonfield,
couldn't be prouder.

00:13:03.150 --> 00:13:05.260
Can't hear us now we'll
yell a little louder."

00:13:05.260 --> 00:13:06.920
I see this cheer is a foreshadowing

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:08.060
of that evening's events,

00:13:08.060 --> 00:13:09.420
where there would be
quite a lot of yelling

00:13:09.420 --> 00:13:11.748
from Haddonfield's student body.

00:13:11.748 --> 00:13:12.581
- Help!

00:13:13.583 --> 00:13:14.833
Help me please!

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:16.533
- The second line,

00:13:16.533 --> 00:13:18.040
"Can't hear us now we'll
yell a little louder"

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:19.680
also fits in nicely with the screams

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:21.760
that finally get Dr. Loomis's attention.

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:23.700
The ground is dry, the ground is dry,

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:25.020
and the ground is wet.

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:27.020
Laurie goes home where we
see, for the only time,

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:28.130
the inside of her room.

00:13:28.130 --> 00:13:29.940
Those of you who are
familiar with my videos

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:31.480
will probably expect me to point out

00:13:31.480 --> 00:13:33.430
this Raggedy Ann doll on her dresser.

00:13:33.430 --> 00:13:35.680
While it is true that this
is the same kind of doll

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:36.950
as the supposedly haunted doll

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:38.420
that inspired the Annabelle movies.

00:13:38.420 --> 00:13:39.840
And while it is also true

00:13:39.840 --> 00:13:42.610
that Ed and Loraine Warren
investigated the doll in 1970,

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:44.550
say, eight years before
Halloween came out,

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:46.470
I'm not entirely sure if John Carpenter

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:48.030
put it in the movie just to be creepy

00:13:48.030 --> 00:13:50.110
or if it was just a
popular doll at the time.

00:13:50.110 --> 00:13:52.470
All I can really say with
certainty is that this doll,

00:13:52.470 --> 00:13:54.801
seen later at the Wallace's
house, is way creepier.

00:13:54.801 --> 00:13:57.550
(scary music)

00:13:57.550 --> 00:14:00.260
Laurie has a large poster of
a painting by James Ensor,

00:14:00.260 --> 00:14:02.580
a Belgian expressionist
painters from the 1800s

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:04.500
who was most famous for
portraying human figures

00:14:04.500 --> 00:14:05.930
wearing grotesque masks.

00:14:05.930 --> 00:14:07.930
That may not seem like
much of a connection today

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:09.890
when masked horror movie
killers are plentiful,

00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:11.740
but you have to remember
when Halloween came out,

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:14.420
Michael Myers wearing a mask
was kind of a novel concept.

00:14:14.420 --> 00:14:16.210
This was the movie that started the trend.

00:14:16.210 --> 00:14:18.130
Laurie looks out the window
and sees Michael standing

00:14:18.130 --> 00:14:19.880
behind some bedsheets on a clothesline.

00:14:19.880 --> 00:14:21.170
There seems to be a running theme

00:14:21.170 --> 00:14:23.320
of Michael appearing behind
sheets and bedsheets,

00:14:23.320 --> 00:14:25.840
which again, all ties into
the psychosexual themes

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:27.550
that I pointed out throughout this video.

00:14:27.550 --> 00:14:29.150
When Annie sees Michael
in the laundry room,

00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:30.010
it's through a sheet

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:31.740
and when he appears to
Lynda in the bedroom,

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:33.610
he's wearing a bedsheet ghost costume.

00:14:33.610 --> 00:14:35.340
So we have all of these associations

00:14:35.340 --> 00:14:37.100
tying Michael's appearances to the idea

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:38.300
of going to bed with someone.

00:14:38.300 --> 00:14:41.080
And since I failed to bring
this up in my "It" 1990 episode,

00:14:41.080 --> 00:14:42.340
I have to mention this here.

00:14:42.340 --> 00:14:44.300
The production designer
and editor of Halloween

00:14:44.300 --> 00:14:45.490
was Tommy Lee Wallace.

00:14:45.490 --> 00:14:47.050
He went on to direct Halloween 3

00:14:47.050 --> 00:14:48.860
but he also went on to direct It,

00:14:48.860 --> 00:14:49.830
where he once again,

00:14:49.830 --> 00:14:52.370
used a line of bedsheets
blowing in the wind for a scare.

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:53.450
Also, it looks like the guy

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:55.410
has like at least three
characters named after him;

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:58.280
Tommy Doyle, Leigh Brackett
and Lindsey Wallace.

00:14:58.280 --> 00:14:59.490
- [JIMMY] That afternoon Loomis heads over

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:01.560
to Judith Myers' grave
site correctly assuming

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:03.330
that Michael will try to
go there at some point.

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:04.460
But unfortunately for him,

00:15:04.460 --> 00:15:06.654
the grave keeper talks his ear off.

00:15:06.654 --> 00:15:10.073
- You know, every town there's
something like this happens.

00:15:11.123 --> 00:15:12.790
I remember over in Russellville.

00:15:12.790 --> 00:15:15.360
Old Charlie Bowles. About 15 years ago.

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:16.510
- He tells the story of a guy

00:15:16.510 --> 00:15:17.940
who went into his garage one night

00:15:17.940 --> 00:15:19.890
and grabbed the hacksaw
and then came inside

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:22.070
and presumably turned his
family into mincemeat.

00:15:22.070 --> 00:15:24.320
Back in 2014, John
Carpenter did an interview

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:25.520
with "Halloween Daily News"

00:15:25.520 --> 00:15:28.370
where he described why he put
this weirdly specific story

00:15:28.370 --> 00:15:29.203
in the movie.

00:15:29.203 --> 00:15:30.850
Apparently, Russellville
was a city in Kentucky

00:15:30.850 --> 00:15:32.400
near where John went to college

00:15:32.400 --> 00:15:33.780
and old Charlie Bowles was named

00:15:33.780 --> 00:15:35.350
after the father of his girlfriend

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:37.050
who was in the Kentucky mafia,

00:15:37.050 --> 00:15:39.300
which I literally just found out existed,

00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:42.020
and apparently, that guy
had her husband whacked.

00:15:42.020 --> 00:15:43.150
In-laws. Am I right?

00:15:43.150 --> 00:15:45.210
Anyway, they find Judith's
grave already uprooted

00:15:45.210 --> 00:15:46.043
when they get there.

00:15:46.043 --> 00:15:47.640
- [Taylor] They'll do
anything for Halloween.

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:49.010
- Guilty as charged.

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:51.250
- Loomis's next stop
is the old Myers house,

00:15:51.250 --> 00:15:54.010
which has gained a reputation
for being supposedly haunted.

00:15:54.010 --> 00:15:56.270
Now, this next one isn't a
traditional thing you missed,

00:15:56.270 --> 00:15:58.930
but it's more something that
nobody talks enough about.

00:15:58.930 --> 00:16:01.290
Loomis and Sheriff Brackett
discovered the remains of a dog

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:02.930
and realized the body is fresh,

00:16:02.930 --> 00:16:05.780
and this is Dr. Loomis's response?

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:07.130
- He got hungry.

00:16:07.130 --> 00:16:09.620
- [ZAC] So Michael got
hungry and pulled a RiceGum?

00:16:09.620 --> 00:16:11.480
This is the most messed
up thing in the movie

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:12.880
and nobody ever talks about it.

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:15.320
So I just had to bring it up and ask, why?

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:17.670
Also, when they go upstairs,
the wallpaper is different

00:16:17.670 --> 00:16:19.150
than it was in 1963.

00:16:19.150 --> 00:16:21.140
We know that nobody lived
there after the Myers

00:16:21.140 --> 00:16:22.870
so that means that Mr. and Mrs. Myers

00:16:22.870 --> 00:16:24.400
actually tried to change the wallpaper

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:26.810
and continue living there
alone after Judith was killed

00:16:26.810 --> 00:16:28.480
and Michael was sent to the sanitarium.

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:29.777
My personal head canon says,

00:16:29.777 --> 00:16:31.410
"They thought they could
get past the trauma

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:33.440
of what happened there if they
change the look of the room,

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:35.040
but ultimately it was too sad

00:16:35.040 --> 00:16:36.990
and they ended up abandoning
the house anyway."

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:38.930
- [JIMMY] That evening, Laurie
and Annie gets settled in

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:40.520
as the two are babysitting at houses

00:16:40.520 --> 00:16:41.930
right across the street from each other.

00:16:41.930 --> 00:16:43.020
And what I can only imagine

00:16:43.020 --> 00:16:45.540
is the 1978 version of iPad parenting,

00:16:45.540 --> 00:16:47.270
Laurie puts a movie on the TV

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:48.840
called The Thing from Another World,

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:50.780
which came out back in 1951.

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:51.613
It's a real movie

00:16:51.613 --> 00:16:53.870
and it was one of John
Carpenter's biggest inspirations.

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:55.920
And it was kind of a preview
of things to come for him

00:16:55.920 --> 00:16:58.890
because in 1982, he would
release his own remake of it,

00:16:58.890 --> 00:17:00.710
which was just shortened to The Thing.

00:17:00.710 --> 00:17:04.000
- Yeah, I think that one
sounds vaguely familiar.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:05.177
- And just like with Halloween,

00:17:05.177 --> 00:17:07.010
Big Trouble in Little China, The Fog,

00:17:07.010 --> 00:17:09.130
and pretty much every
other John Carpenter movie,

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:11.610
when it came out, it was a
critical and commercial bomb,

00:17:11.610 --> 00:17:13.200
but then as the decades went on,

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:14.900
it became revered as a classic.

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:16.350
Can you see why all John Carpenter

00:17:16.350 --> 00:17:17.770
really seems to want to do these days

00:17:17.770 --> 00:17:19.580
is sit inside and play Fallout?

00:17:19.580 --> 00:17:20.670
- Why the glum face?

00:17:20.670 --> 00:17:23.180
I thought random murder
was routine around here.

00:17:23.180 --> 00:17:24.630
- [ZAC] As Laurie and
Annie talk on the phone

00:17:24.630 --> 00:17:26.550
about the possibility of
attending the homecoming dance

00:17:26.550 --> 00:17:27.720
with her crush, Ben Tramer,

00:17:27.720 --> 00:17:30.110
Michael appears outside the
house for the first time.

00:17:30.110 --> 00:17:31.010
At the beginning of the movie,

00:17:31.010 --> 00:17:33.010
we saw Michael spying on
a teenage relationship

00:17:33.010 --> 00:17:33.860
from his point of view,

00:17:33.860 --> 00:17:35.310
but now we're seeing the other side

00:17:35.310 --> 00:17:37.280
as Tommy notices The Shape
is standing in the dark

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:38.870
outside of the Wallace's house.

00:17:38.870 --> 00:17:40.400
- Michael's many background appearances

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:41.950
are one of the things
that makes this movie

00:17:41.950 --> 00:17:43.910
so fun to revisit year after year.

00:17:43.910 --> 00:17:45.700
Every time they release
it on a new medium,

00:17:45.700 --> 00:17:47.940
like DVD, Blu-ray, or 4k Blu-ray,

00:17:47.940 --> 00:17:50.100
someone manages to find
him in a different spot

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:51.000
and I love that.

00:17:51.000 --> 00:17:52.980
- Yeah, Jimmy, I can't wait
until you see this movie

00:17:52.980 --> 00:17:55.010
after you upgrade from
seeing it on LaserDisc.

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:56.980
Just as bedsheets have served as a link

00:17:56.980 --> 00:17:59.640
between Michael and the
things we do in the dark,

00:17:59.640 --> 00:18:01.900
well, darkness is also a reminder

00:18:01.900 --> 00:18:03.470
of that thing we do in the dark.

00:18:03.470 --> 00:18:04.900
Now you're probably
reaching for that button

00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:05.860
to click off this video

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:08.310
because almost all horror
movies take place in the dark.

00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:09.143
Right?

00:18:09.143 --> 00:18:10.360
Well, hold on just a minute.

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:11.860
It's not just the darkness here.

00:18:11.860 --> 00:18:14.410
Although lighting wise,
this movie is really dark.

00:18:14.410 --> 00:18:16.990
There are scenes where the
screen is almost entirely black.

00:18:16.990 --> 00:18:18.250
You could say this is just a result

00:18:18.250 --> 00:18:20.480
of this being a low budget
horror movie shot on film,

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:22.220
but it's not just a couple of scenes.

00:18:22.220 --> 00:18:24.580
There are a lot of scenes
with barely any lights.

00:18:24.580 --> 00:18:27.870
Two, the darkness is acknowledged
in the dialogue by Lynda.

00:18:27.870 --> 00:18:30.110
- Hey, it's totally dark.
- Yeah.

00:18:30.110 --> 00:18:33.280
- And three, what happens in
the dark is also relevant here.

00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:35.290
For example, the screams in this movie.

00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:36.190
They aren't your typical

00:18:36.190 --> 00:18:37.860
blood curdling horror movie screams.

00:18:37.860 --> 00:18:41.303
They almost sound more
like something else.

00:18:42.790 --> 00:18:45.989
(girl squealing)
(car horn blaring)

00:18:45.989 --> 00:18:47.145
(ominous music)

00:18:47.145 --> 00:18:48.438
(girl squealing)

00:18:48.438 --> 00:18:51.830
(closet rattling)
(girl squealing)

00:18:51.830 --> 00:18:53.880
When Laurie hears Annie trying
to scream over the phone,

00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:55.240
she even kind of acknowledges

00:18:55.240 --> 00:18:57.810
that it sounds like there were
some extra-curricular activities

00:18:57.810 --> 00:18:58.643
going on.

00:19:00.138 --> 00:19:01.270
- All right, Annie.

00:19:01.270 --> 00:19:02.790
First, I get your famous chewing,

00:19:02.790 --> 00:19:04.400
now I get your famous squealing.

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:05.580
- That's not even all of them.

00:19:05.580 --> 00:19:06.740
Next time you watch the movie,

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:08.220
that's something you can look out for.

00:19:08.220 --> 00:19:10.660
But it's not just the
screams, I'm also gonna look

00:19:10.660 --> 00:19:12.390
at how some of these kills are executed

00:19:12.390 --> 00:19:14.190
and I'll be analyzing what the ending

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:17.582
of John Carpenter's
Halloween really represents.

00:19:17.582 --> 00:19:22.582
(ominous music)
(thunder rumbling)

00:19:22.670 --> 00:19:25.337
(ominous music)

00:19:26.540 --> 00:19:28.660
Keeping on topic with my main thesis

00:19:28.660 --> 00:19:29.790
about Michael's rampage,

00:19:29.790 --> 00:19:32.410
being a release of distorted
psychosexual attention,

00:19:32.410 --> 00:19:34.680
let's look at the death scenes
of each of Laurie's friends

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:36.640
and see how they tie in to this idea.

00:19:36.640 --> 00:19:39.240
The first to go is the Sheriff's
daughter, Annie Brackett.

00:19:39.240 --> 00:19:41.110
Michael ambushes her when
she hops into the car

00:19:41.110 --> 00:19:42.170
to go pick up her boyfriend,

00:19:42.170 --> 00:19:43.540
but she realizes something is wrong

00:19:43.540 --> 00:19:45.450
when she notices that the
windows are fogged up,

00:19:45.450 --> 00:19:47.370
a sign that somebody had
been breathing inside

00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:49.340
of the enclosed airspace for some time.

00:19:49.340 --> 00:19:51.280
Anyone who had a high
school or college girlfriend

00:19:51.280 --> 00:19:52.980
is probably familiar with the concept

00:19:52.980 --> 00:19:54.370
of the fogged up car window.

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:56.680
Since teens usually don't have
a good place to get intimate,

00:19:56.680 --> 00:19:58.070
they often end up driving a car

00:19:58.070 --> 00:20:00.130
to a secluded location for some privacy.

00:20:00.130 --> 00:20:01.840
So it's probably an intentional choice

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:03.310
for Michael to replicate that,

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:04.530
and the fact that she was on her way

00:20:04.530 --> 00:20:05.440
to pick up her boyfriend

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:07.940
certainly fits with the
pattern of Michael's attacks.

00:20:07.940 --> 00:20:10.310
- Someone smart like Laurie
probably would have realized

00:20:10.310 --> 00:20:11.960
that somebody is in the car.

00:20:11.960 --> 00:20:13.187
- [Zac] The next victims would be Lynda

00:20:13.187 --> 00:20:14.630
and her boyfriend, Bob.

00:20:14.630 --> 00:20:16.980
It really bothers me how Bob
just leaves the car door open

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:18.550
when they first pull up to the house.

00:20:18.550 --> 00:20:19.800
- Yeah, why does he do that?

00:20:19.800 --> 00:20:21.930
- The way he stalks
them is very reminiscent

00:20:21.930 --> 00:20:24.630
of Michael's childhood Halloween in 1963.

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:26.040
He watches them make out on the couch

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:27.350
and he continues to spy on them

00:20:27.350 --> 00:20:29.010
after they move things upstairs.

00:20:29.010 --> 00:20:31.090
Notice his shadow passing by right here.

00:20:31.090 --> 00:20:32.440
In a break from their activities,

00:20:32.440 --> 00:20:34.560
Bob goes to the kitchen
to get a couple of drinks

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:35.680
and Michael uses a knife

00:20:35.680 --> 00:20:37.350
to literally pin him up to the wall,

00:20:37.350 --> 00:20:40.360
which symbolizes another
teenage cliche: pin-up models.

00:20:40.360 --> 00:20:42.930
If you're somehow unfamiliar,
a pin-up is just a poster

00:20:42.930 --> 00:20:44.390
of an attractive model or celebrity

00:20:44.390 --> 00:20:46.290
that teens like to pin up
onto their bedroom wall

00:20:46.290 --> 00:20:48.450
so they can fantasize about
them as they go to sleep.

00:20:48.450 --> 00:20:50.380
The last kill of the movie is Lynda.

00:20:50.380 --> 00:20:52.460
When Michael approaches her
and he's wearing a costume,

00:20:52.460 --> 00:20:54.860
which leads her to believe that
this is her boyfriend, Bob.

00:20:54.860 --> 00:20:56.980
This one represents role-play.

00:20:56.980 --> 00:21:01.210
- What's the matter? Can't I
get your ghost, Bob? (laughs)

00:21:01.210 --> 00:21:02.920
- Maybe it's cause I was born in the 90s,

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:04.610
but I still don't get why that's funny.

00:21:04.610 --> 00:21:05.950
I think there's also something to be said

00:21:05.950 --> 00:21:07.280
about how easy it is for Michael

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:08.450
to trap and kill these three

00:21:08.450 --> 00:21:10.560
who were established to be the
most likely to be distracted

00:21:10.560 --> 00:21:11.790
by the allure of hooking up,

00:21:11.790 --> 00:21:14.110
as opposed to Lori who is
much more level-headed.

00:21:14.110 --> 00:21:15.930
At times, it feels like Michael Myers

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:17.370
almost doesn't want to kill her.

00:21:17.370 --> 00:21:19.350
It seems like he intentionally
misses her right here

00:21:19.350 --> 00:21:20.550
and cuts her shirt instead.

00:21:20.550 --> 00:21:22.170
Then again later, she's not moving,

00:21:22.170 --> 00:21:24.210
yet he sticks the knife
into the couch instead.

00:21:24.210 --> 00:21:26.040
In the context of the
message of the movie,

00:21:26.040 --> 00:21:27.410
it's like he's giving her a warning.

00:21:27.410 --> 00:21:28.780
He knows she has dirty thoughts

00:21:28.780 --> 00:21:30.770
as established in the
car early that afternoon,

00:21:30.770 --> 00:21:32.590
but her mind isn't consumed by dating

00:21:32.590 --> 00:21:33.700
and boys like the others.

00:21:33.700 --> 00:21:35.380
So taking her out doesn't really fit in

00:21:35.380 --> 00:21:36.400
with Michael's agenda.

00:21:36.400 --> 00:21:39.010
The chase continues on to
the Doyle's upstairs bedroom.

00:21:39.010 --> 00:21:40.790
Laurie thinks that she's
gotten the better of Michael,

00:21:40.790 --> 00:21:41.623
but in the background,

00:21:41.623 --> 00:21:43.470
he can be seen rising up once more

00:21:43.470 --> 00:21:45.620
in a manner very similar to Nosferatu

00:21:45.620 --> 00:21:48.440
from the 1922 classic German horror movie.

00:21:48.440 --> 00:21:50.960
Some might say there's a
phallic parallel going on here.

00:21:50.960 --> 00:21:53.500
Loomis shows up in buries some
lead into his former patient

00:21:53.500 --> 00:21:54.640
knocking him off the balcony.

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:56.040
But when he goes to collect his spoils,

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:58.090
the mysterious villain has disappeared.

00:21:58.090 --> 00:22:00.090
The movie ends as the iconic score

00:22:00.090 --> 00:22:02.390
shows us various locations
all around the house

00:22:02.390 --> 00:22:04.620
accompanied by the slow
unnerving breathing

00:22:04.620 --> 00:22:06.040
of the evil antagonist.

00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:08.120
The final shot takes us back once more

00:22:08.120 --> 00:22:09.990
to the Myers house as if to suggest

00:22:09.990 --> 00:22:11.390
that this entire series of events

00:22:11.390 --> 00:22:13.200
was just another mental episode.

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:15.420
Just like the one that
young Michael Myers went on

00:22:15.420 --> 00:22:17.140
all of those years ago.

00:22:17.140 --> 00:22:19.300
- I think the reason
Halloween has endured so long

00:22:19.300 --> 00:22:20.300
is because at its heart,

00:22:20.300 --> 00:22:22.290
it's a simple story
about a local boogeyman

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:24.490
coming back to haunt a
town on Halloween night

00:22:24.490 --> 00:22:26.270
that could be any town in America.

00:22:26.270 --> 00:22:28.110
The decision to have him
escape into the night

00:22:28.110 --> 00:22:29.780
at the end of the movie was so smart

00:22:29.780 --> 00:22:31.890
because if he's alive,
that means he's out there

00:22:31.890 --> 00:22:33.790
and he could come to your
town on Halloween night

00:22:33.790 --> 00:22:35.310
and you could have a run in with him.

00:22:35.310 --> 00:22:37.180
That's what kept me up
at night when I was a kid

00:22:37.180 --> 00:22:39.110
and some of the revelations in Halloween 2

00:22:39.110 --> 00:22:40.290
also kept me up at night,

00:22:40.290 --> 00:22:42.090
but for completely different reasons.

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:43.910
